{"step_index":1454,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-05T14:38:52Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-05T08:38:52-06:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-05T08:38:52-06:00\nShowing lines 1-37 of 37\n1: # ==============================================================================\n2: # CONFIGURACIÓN DE ENTORNO U-BOOT (env) — ZTE ZXV10 B866V2 (Placa: franklin / g12a)\n3: # Procesador: Amlogic S905X2 | Memoria: 2GB DDR4 + 8GB eMMC | Wi-Fi: MT7661LSN\n4: # ==============================================================================\n5: \n6: baudrate=115200\n7: board=franklin\n8: soc=g12a\n9: arch=arm\n10: cpu=armv8\n11: \n12: # Parámetros de salida de video HDMI y espacio de color (Amlogic VOUT / HDMITX)\n13: hdmimode=1080p60hz\n14: outputmode=1080p60hz\n15: colorattribute=444,8bit\n16: dolby_status=0\n17: hdr_policy=1\n18: frac_rate_policy=1\n19: \n20: # Resolución del Framebuffer de interfaz (OSD)\n21: fb_width=1920\n22: fb_height=1080\n23: display_bpp=32\n24: display_color_index=32\n25: display_layer=osd0\n26: \n27: # Argumentos pasados al Kernel Linux (cmdline)\n28: initargs=rootfstype=ramfs init=/init console=ttyS0,115200 no_console_suspend earlycon=aml-uart,0xff803000 ramoops.pstore_en=1 ramoops.record_size=0x8000 ramoops.console_size=0x4000\n29: \n30: # Comandos de arranque estándar (Android 12 en eMMC)\n31: storeboot=imgread kernel boot ${loadaddr}; bootm ${loadaddr};\n32: \n33: # Configuración de Multiboot por USB / SD (Armbian / Debian / CoreELEC / EmuELEC)\n34: # Basado en mirko-geno/b866v2-debian y ophub/amlogic-s9xxx-armbian\n35: start_autoscript=if usb start; then run start_usb_autoscript; fi; if mmcinfo; then run start_mmc_autoscript; fi;\n36: start_usb_autoscript=if fatload usb 0 1020000 s905_autoscript; then autoscr 1020000; fi; if fatload usb 1 1020000 s905_autoscript; then autoscr 1020000; fi;\n37: start_mmc_autoscript=if fatload mmc 0 1020000 s905_autoscript; then autoscr 1020000; fi;"}
{"step_index":1455,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-05T14:38:52Z","input_tokens":3030,"cache_read_tokens":102945,"output_tokens":2717,"thinking":"U-Boot's behavior with a corrupted or erased environment block is now the focus. The handling of `/dev/block/env`'s potential invalid CRC32 or zeroed content is under scrutiny. Examining U-Boot's environment management source code to understand error handling in these scenarios is critical.\n\n\nU-Boot's boot process involves reading the environment from the specified block device and verifying its CRC. An invalid CRC triggers loading of the compiled-in default environment. This default environment is then immediately saved to the block device. The implication is that a corrupted or erased environment block is automatically overwritten by the default.\n\n\nThe boot process shows environment saving to `aml-storage`, at offset `0x45c00000`. This aligns with the previous understanding of an environment write upon boot. However, the `ztebsp` messages indicate an image header mismatch after the environment save, possibly indicating a subsequent boot image corruption issue.\n\n\nThe U-Boot boot process uses the compiled-in default environment when the stored environment block is corrupt. This default environment, containing ZTE's original settings, is then written back to the environment block upon boot. This process effectively regenerates the genuine ZTE environment, even after erasure, as the default is always saved after loading. Sending `defenv` via `adnl.exe` ensures the regeneration of the original ZTE environment before further actions, like flashing the recovery image, are taken.\n\n\nThe `bootcmd` environment variable is the key to executing the desired boot sequence, set to `bootsys`. The plan involves setting an unlock code and saving the environment to ensure an unlocked bootloader state. The reboot then triggers the U-Boot sequence, including HDMI initialization, logo display, and kernel loading. It boots directly into the `DMC.v2.25` (Android 12) system.\n\nConsidering the `env_unlocked.img` file, ensuring it contains the correct environment variables is critical. Variables like `loadaddr`, `bootcmd`, and `preboot` need to be explicitly set within the image. It is also important to prevent mistakenly flashing `env_unlocked.img` over the active `env` partition during the flashing process.\n\n\nThe `fastboot oem` commands confirm the intent to set the default environment and then unlock. The user's box is currently in a state where the unlocked environment image is loaded. This causes U-Boot to immediately attempt to update via USB upon power-on. The device enumerates in DNL mode after the update command executes, which should be within seconds. The prolonged connection in DNL mode from the user's box is puzzling and warrants further investigation.\n\n\nThe prolonged DNL mode enumeration is the first major clue. The device was connected for over six minutes before the first `getvar identify` command. The subsequent loss of USB connection, occurring after the initial `getvar` and before the retry, suggests either a user-initiated disconnect or a U-Boot watchdog reset due to USB inactivity. The timestamps show the connection dropping. This could indicate a power interruption or reset, possibly during a critical operation. It's imperative to determine the cause of this disconnect.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"Get-PnpDevice -PresentOnly | Where-Object { $_.InstanceId -match '1B8E|18D1|19D2' } | Format-Table Status, Class, FriendlyName, InstanceId -AutoSize\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolSummary":"\"Check if any box USB device is currently present\""}}]}
