{"step_index":1463,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-05T14:40:13Z","input_tokens":2990,"cache_read_tokens":109825,"output_tokens":1055,"thinking":"The `adnl.exe` strings reveal the ability to send a \"BL33\" string, suggesting a potential command injection vulnerability. U-Boot's `run_command_list` function within `BL33` interprets semicolon-separated commands, implying a way to execute arbitrary U-Boot commands through a crafted \"oem\" string. This strongly indicates a potential command injection vector.\n\n\nThe crafted command string aims to restore the original ZTE bootloader environment and disable any lock mechanisms. It first resets to the default environment, followed by setting the 'lock' variable and explicitly setting `loadaddr` and `bootcmd` to specific values. Finally, it removes any upgrade flags and saves these settings to persist the changes.\n\n\nThe boot process initiates a reset, initializes HDMI to 1080p60hz, displays the boot logo, executes `bootsys`, loads and decompresses the kernel, and finally boots the Android system. Considering potential U-Boot command execution, I must investigate how the command parser handles errors and ensure that even if `defenv` fails, subsequent commands are still executed. I will also check the structure of a genuine ZTE environment to confirm variables and settings.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"Select-String -Path \\\"c:\\\\Users\\\\andre\\\\OneDrive\\\\Desktop\\\\xview\\\\experimento\\\\*\\\\*\\\\*\\\" -Pattern \\\"init_display|storeboot|bootsys|0x03080000\\\" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object -First 40 Path, LineNumber, Line\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolSummary":"\"Search experimento folder for init_display, storeboot, bootsys, and 0x03080000\""}}]}
